VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-a11y
FAMILY: anthropic
TASK: W2
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Scope: git diff master -- web/templates/components/whatif/portfolio-settings.html
       web/templates/components/whatif/quick-adjust-scripts.html
Standard: ACCESSIBILITY.md (repo) + WCAG 2.2 AA.
No automated pipeline (axe-core/CLI) is wired for this Go-template repo (no
package.json / npm harness present) — audit is manual, tracing the shipped
JS, per the checker brief's own admission that automated results are
"necessary but not sufficient" here they are simply absent, so 100% of the
weight is on the manual walk below.

1. LABEL ASSOCIATION / KEYBOARD OPERABILITY — PASS (pre-existing gap noted)
   - `<label>` at portfolio-settings.html:53 has no `for=` and does not wrap
     the input, on both master and this branch — UNCHANGED by this diff.
     Confirmed via `git show master:...` (identical `<label class="...">
     Monthly Living Expenses</label>` with no `for`). This is a real
     ACCESSIBILITY.md #4 / WCAG 4.1.2 gap but it is PRE-EXISTING, not
     introduced by W2. Not counted against this attempt; flagged as an
     OBSERVATION for a future task.
   - The visible `<input type="range" id="monthly_living_expenses_input">`
     (line 64) keeps no explicit tabindex/disabled changes — still native,
     still keyboard-operable (arrow keys fire native `input` events exactly
     as mouse drag does).
   - Traced the JS path for both mouse and keyboard: range `oninput` →
     `onMonthlyLivingExpensesInput(this.value)` (portfolio-settings.html
     line ~182) sets `#monthly_living_expenses_value` (hidden, `name=
     monthly_living_expenses`) and dispatches a bubbling `input` Event on
     it → document-level capture listener in quick-adjust-scripts.html
     (`document.addEventListener('input', ..., true)`) sees
     `data-quick-adjust-key` on the hidden field → `syncQuickAdjustKey(
     'monthly_living_expenses')` → updates
     `#monthly_living_expenses_display` (span, visible) and mirror controls.
     Keyboard changes DO reach the submitted hidden field and DO reach the
     visible display. PASS.

2. NOTE TEXT CONTRAST — pre-existing FAIL, not newly introduced
   New `<p id="living-expenses-phase-note" class="text-xs text-gray-400
   dark:text-gray-500 mt-1">` (portfolio-settings.html:72) reuses the exact
   class string already on the untouched caption at line 70 ("In today's
   dollars...") and lines 83/91 (property-tax captions) — confirmed byte-
   identical in `git show master:...`.
   Computed contrast (sRGB relative luminance, WCAG formula):
     - Light theme: text-gray-400 #9CA3AF on bg-white #FFFFFF → 2.54:1
       (needs 4.5:1 for this 12px/text-xs text). FAIL against WCAG 1.4.3
       and ACCESSIBILITY.md #7.
     - Dark theme: dark:text-gray-500 #6B7280 on dark:bg-gray-800 #1F2937
       → 3.04:1. Also FAILS 4.5:1. ACCESSIBILITY.md #12 (dark-mode parity)
       is therefore also unmet, but symmetrically with light mode — no new
       theme-specific gap.
   Both ratios are IDENTICAL to the pre-existing caption already shipping
   on this exact page. This is a real, sitewide AA contrast bug but it is
   NOT newly introduced by W2 — the new note just inherits it by reusing
   the class. Not counted as a new failure for this task; flagged as an
   OBSERVATION (recommend bumping to text-gray-500/dark:text-gray-400, or
   darker, across all four captions in one follow-up, not scoped here).

3. DYNAMIC UPDATE ANNOUNCEMENT — PASS (consistent with existing pattern)
   `updateLivingExpensesPhaseNote()` rewrites only `#living-expenses-phase-
   amount`'s textContent on drag; no `aria-live` region wraps it or any of
   the page's other quick-adjust display spans (`#monthly_living_expenses_
   display`, the portfolio-value span, the projection-years span — grepped,
   none carry aria-live). The one `aria-live="polite"` region in this
   template family (conversion-sweep.html:43) is for an operation-result
   status message, a different category. Treating the phase-note figure as
   a visual-only live preview matches the page's established convention for
   every other slider readout, so this is not a new deviation and not an
   ACCESSIBILITY.md #16 violation (no affordance is being hidden from AT —
   the static sentence structure, phase name, and multiplier are always
   present in the accessible tree; only the numeral inside updates, exactly
   like every other slider's display span already does without a live
   region). OBSERVATION only, not blocking: a11y best practice would still
   argue for `aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"` on figures like this
   that are not adjacent-in-DOM to the control driving them, but WCAG 2.2 AA
   does not mandate it and the constitution doesn't either.

4. MULTIPLICATION SIGN / SEMICOLON LINEARIZATION — PASS, OBSERVATION
   Rendered sentence (from living_expenses_phase_note.go):
     "Engine spends $8,140.00/mo now (Go-Go ×1.1); ×0.9 from age 70."
   Linearizes as plain text; no content or order is lost when read
   serially. The "×" (U+00D7) glyph's pronunciation is screen-reader/
   verbosity-setting dependent (NVDA symbol dictionary says "times"; some
   configurations at low symbol-verbosity may elide it), which could
   collapse "Go-Go ×1.1" to "Go-Go 1.1" — still parseable in context, not a
   WCAG failure (no SC mandates a specific glyph-to-word mapping), but
   worth a follow-up to spell out "x" or "times" for robustness. OBSERVATION,
   non-blocking.

5. HIDDEN INPUT PHANTOM CONTROL — PASS
   `<input type="hidden" id="monthly_living_expenses_value" ...>` — hidden
   inputs are categorically excluded from the accessibility tree and the
   tab order per the HTML AAM; confirmed no other attribute (role, aria-*,
   tabindex) overrides that here. No phantom control introduced.

6. SLIDER VALUE ANNOUNCEMENT vs DISPLAYED VALUE — FAIL (newly surfaced
   asymmetry; AA-relevant)
   Scenario: a saved value off the $100 grid (e.g. 7386, as the phase-
   visibility work's own snap-trap fix explicitly calls out — "e.g. 7386,
   from 'Sync from Dashboard'", portfolio-settings.html:57-58).
   Per the HTML Standard's range-input value sanitization algorithm, an
   off-step `value` attribute is clamped AND step-snapped to the nearest
   valid step as soon as the browser parses it — this happens to the
   range's own DOM `.value` (and therefore its implicit `aria-valuenow`,
   since `<input type=range>` exposes ARIA state straight from that
   property) regardless of whether the user ever touches the control.
   7386 with min=0/step=100 snaps to 7400 (nearest step, 14 away vs 86 away
   for 7300).
   - Sighted users see: `#monthly_living_expenses_display` = "$7,386"
     (server-rendered from the exact float, line 69) and the new phase
     note computed from that same exact figure.
   - The saved/submitted value is: exactly 7386.00 (hidden field, never
     browser-sanitized because type=hidden isn't subject to range
     sanitization) — this is the whole point of the W2 fix.
   - What AT gets for the range control itself (role=slider, native
     aria-valuenow derived from the range's own sanitized `.value`): 7400.
   This is a real, measurable $14 divergence between what a screen-reader
   user is told the control holds and what every other channel (visual
   display, phase note, submitted data) says. It is not raw new math — the
   underlying browser step-snapping quirk predates this diff — but the
   CONSEQUENCE changed in a way that matters for the ruling: before W2, the
   range was also the canonical `name=monthly_living_expenses` field, so
   the same snap corrupted the submitted/saved value too (that's the
   "snap trap" being fixed) — sighted and AT users were both told a wrong
   but AT LEAST mutually consistent number. After W2, sighted users and the
   saved data are fixed to the correct exact figure, while the range
   control's own accessible value is left pointing at the old, now-provably
   wrong, snapped number. The fix introduces a new divergence between the
   AT channel and every other channel for the exact control a screen reader
   user is interacting with.
   Ruling: this is a WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value) violation — "value...
   can be programmatically determined" is met literally (aria-valuenow is
   present) but not met in the sense that matters: the determined value is
   wrong relative to the actual, submitted, sighted-visible state of the
   same control. It is also an ACCESSIBILITY.md #16-style parity gap in
   spirit (the accessibility tree asserting a number the page itself no
   longer stands behind), even though #16 is literally scoped to
   suppression/dismissal rather than value drift — cite 4.1.2 as the
   binding criterion, #16 as the constitutional principle this rhymes with.
   FIX REQUIRED: add `aria-valuetext` to the visible range, kept in sync
   with the exact hidden value (e.g. set alongside the existing
   `updateLivingExpensesPhaseNote`/`syncQuickAdjustDisplays` calls, and set
   once server-side at initial render from `.Settings.MonthlyLivingExpenses`
   so the very-first paint — before any interaction — is also correct for
   AT, since the snap already applies pre-interaction). Until
   `aria-valuetext` (or an equivalent live correction) exists, a screen-
   reader user editing this control never hears the number that is actually
   saved or shown to sighted users whenever the saved value is off the
   $100 grid.

Net: five of six checks pass or are pre-existing/non-blocking; check 6 is a
newly-introduced, citable AA (4.1.2) failure directly caused by decoupling
the accessible/native range control from the exact value it used to (badly)
share with the form submission. VERDICT: FAIL. Re-verify after
`aria-valuetext` (or equivalent) is added and kept live-synced.
